"Lasers and Stimulated Emission: teaching notes",
"Lasers and Stimulated Emission",
"Modern physics",
"Light interacts with a two level atom in three ways: absorption, spontaneous emission (random direction and phase) and stimulated emission, which makes a copy of the incoming photon. Absorption and stimulated emission have the same coefficient, B₁₂ = B₂₁, so which wins depends only on whether N₁ or N₂ is larger.",
"In thermal equilibrium N₂/N₁ = e^(−hν/kT) < {#} at every temperature, and pumping a two level atom can only bring N₂ up to N₁. Gain needs a population inversion, which needs a third or fourth level.",
"A laser starts when the gain from the inversion balances the cavity losses. Above that threshold the inversion stays clamped at N_th and the extra pump power comes out as laser light, in a straight line against pump power.",
"A four level laser (Nd:YAG) has an empty lower level; a three level laser (ruby) must first excite half its ions, so its threshold is far higher.",
"The cavity allows only frequencies ν_q = qc/2L. The modes that fall inside the Doppler broadened gain curve, above threshold, are the ones that lase.",
"Students should know E = hν, the Boltzmann factor and energy levels. There are four views, <strong>Processes</strong>, <strong>Laser</strong>, <strong>Energy levels</strong> and <strong>Modes</strong>; each preset opens the one it needs. The <strong>Laser</strong> view shows the cavity, a time trace, and output and inversion against pump power; the pump grip and the output mirror can be dragged. <strong>Switch on again</strong> restarts the laser with the pump just switched on.",
"<strong>Absorption vs stimulated emission.</strong> Walk through the three panels: absorption, spontaneous emission in a random direction, and stimulated emission of a copy traveling with the incoming photon. Ask Q1 here.",
"<strong>No laser without inversion.</strong> Drag the <strong>Temperature T</strong> up to {#}⁵ K and watch <strong>Thermal N₂/N₁</strong> approach {#} but never pass it. Then drag <strong>Pump light, W/A₂₁</strong>: the pumped two level atom also stays below {#} (Q2, Q3).",
"<strong>Threshold.</strong> In the <strong>Laser</strong> view, drag the operating point along the output curve. Below threshold the output is only spontaneous emission, far too small to see; above it the output rises in a straight line while <strong>Inversion now, N/N_th</strong> stays at {#} (Q4).",
"<strong>Turning on: relaxation oscillations.</strong> The pump switches on at t = {#}. Ask the class why nothing happens for the first {#} µs (Q5). Then the light overshoots in a train of spikes that ring down to the steady output. Press <strong>Switch on again</strong> to repeat.",
"<strong>Three level vs four level.</strong> Ruby in the same cavity, with Nd:YAG dashed. Compare the threshold readouts, then open <strong>Energy levels</strong> to see why (Q6).",
"<strong>Cavity modes of a He–Ne laser.</strong> A {#} cm cavity has modes every {#} MHz inside a gain curve about {#} GHz wide. Drag the comb (<strong>Fine change of L</strong>) and watch modes hop in and out; then shorten <strong>Mirror spacing L</strong> (Q7).",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: a beam of light at the transition frequency passes through a gas of two level atoms, half of them in the upper level and half in the lower. Does the beam get brighter, dimmer, or stay the same? What if {#}% are in the upper level?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> The He–Ne laser line is at {#} nm. Find the photon energy in eV and the ratio N₂/N₁ for neon atoms in thermal equilibrium at {#} K. At what temperature would N₂/N₁ = ½? Compare with the CO₂ line at {#} µm at {#} K. Check your values in <strong>Processes</strong>.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> A two level atom is pumped with light at its own transition frequency. Explain why N₂ can approach N₁ but never exceed it, however strong the pump. What would the medium do to a weak probe beam when N₂ ≈ N₁?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> In <strong>Threshold</strong>, the Nd:YAG laser starts at about {#} W of absorbed pump power and gives {#} W of output at {#} W absorbed. Find the slope efficiency, ΔP_out/ΔP_abs. Explain why it is less than {#}, using the pump and laser wavelengths ({#} and {#} nm) and the two cavity losses: the output mirror (R = {#}) and {#}% internal loss per round trip. Why does the inversion stop growing above threshold?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> In <strong>Turning on: relaxation oscillations</strong>, the pump is switched on at twice threshold and the first spike appears about {#} µs later. The upper level lifetime is {#} µs. Without lasing, the inversion would rise as N(t) ≈ N_∞({#} − e^(−t/τ)) toward N_∞ ≈ 2N_th. Estimate when it crosses threshold. Why does the light then overshoot instead of rising smoothly to its steady value?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> In <strong>Three level vs four level</strong>, the threshold inversion for ruby is about {#}% of all Cr³⁺ ions. What fraction of the ions must be in the upper level at threshold? Why does Nd:YAG need almost none of its ions excited, and why does that make its threshold pump power several times lower?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> For the He–Ne cavity, find the mode spacing c/2L for L = {#} cm. With a gain curve {#} GHz wide (above threshold over its full width at half maximum), how many modes lase? How short must the cavity be for only one mode to fit? Check by changing <strong>Mirror spacing L</strong> and dragging <strong>Fine change of L</strong>.",
"<strong>\"Stimulated emission is a rare, special process.\"</strong> It has the same coefficient as absorption; it is hidden only because the lower level is so much more populated.",
"<strong>\"The laser photons come from the pump light.\"</strong> The pump only fills the upper level. The laser photons are made by stimulated emission on another transition, at a longer wavelength.",
"<strong>\"More pump power means more inversion.\"</strong> Above threshold the inversion is clamped at N_th, and the extra power goes into the beam.",
"<strong>Processes:</strong> one transition with equal degeneracies (B₁₂ = B₂₁); the thermal ratio is the Boltzmann factor, and stimulated over spontaneous, {#}/(e^(hν/kT) − {#}), uses the Planck energy density. The pumped atom obeys dN₂/dt = W(N₁ − N₂) − A₂₁N₂ with kT ≪ hν. Animations are not to scale.",
"<strong>Laser:</strong> rate equations for the inversion N and the photon number q in one cavity mode of a uniform medium (no hole burning, no thermal effects); τ_c = t_rt/(−ln R − ln {#}), N_th = {#}/(Kτ_c), K = σc/(AL). Ideal four level Nd:YAG and three level ruby: pump bands and the Nd lower level empty instantly. {#} cm rod, {#} mm mode radius, {#} cm cavity, β = {#}⁻⁹ of spontaneous emission into the mode. Real ruby lasers are flash pumped in pulses.",
"The <strong>Relaxation oscillation</strong> readout is the linearized result about the steady state. The first large spikes in the trace are spaced more widely than its period (about {#} µs against {#} µs at twice threshold) and approach it only as they die down.",
